What was changed
----------------

  * Windows support (the reason for the fork). Upstream v0.1.26/0.2.0-alpha
    fails to build on Windows (~19 errors: Unix-only deps and code). The daemon
    IPC layer — over which EVERY `bd` command is served (the CLI is a thin IPC
    client that auto-starts the daemon; there is no in-process path) — was
    Unix-domain-socket-only. It now builds and runs on Windows:

      - A cross-platform Unix-domain-socket alias, `beads_surface::ipc::uds`:
        `std::os::unix::net` on unix, the `uds_windows` crate on Windows
        (Windows 10 1803+ supports AF_UNIX with filesystem paths, so the
        socket-path logic is unchanged). The daemon server (beads-daemon
        run.rs/server) and the IPC client (beads-surface) use this alias.
      - Process liveness / termination: unix uses `nix` `kill(pid, 0)` /
        SIGTERM/SIGKILL; Windows uses the Win32 process API
        (`beads_surface::ipc::proc_util`, via `windows-sys`). `beads-daemon`,
        which is `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`, delegates to beads-surface for this.
      - Store advisory locking: unix keeps `nix::fcntl::Flock`; Windows adds a
        `LockFileEx`-backed shim (`beads-daemon .../store/win_lock.rs`, via
        `fs2`) exposing the same `Flock`/`FlockArg` API (unlock-on-drop).
      - Daemon autostart spawn: unix keeps the `posix_spawn` fd-sanitizer;
        Windows spawns via `CreateProcessW` with `bInheritHandles = FALSE`
        (detached, no window) so the long-lived daemon does not hold the
        client's stdio/pipe handles open — the moral equivalent of the unix
        fd-sanitizer, and required for `x=$(bd ...)` / `bd ... | grep` to work.
      - WAL durability: `fsync_dir` (open-the-directory-and-fsync) is a unix
        idiom Windows rejects (`ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED` on a directory handle); it
        is a no-op on Windows (NTFS journals directory-entry changes).
      - `git2` builds `vendored-openssl` only on unix; on Windows libgit2 uses
        the native WinHTTP/SChannel backend. `rusqlite` uses the `bundled`
        SQLite so no system libsqlite3 is required.
      - POSIX signals (SIGTERM/SIGINT graceful shutdown), the `nix`, `libc`, and
        `signal-hook` dependencies, and `std::os::unix` usage in `src/upgrade.rs`
        are all `#[cfg(unix)]`-gated. `nix`/`libc`/`signal-hook` are declared as
        `[target.'cfg(unix)'.dependencies]`.

  * Android/Termux: all platform gating uses `cfg(unix)` (which includes
    `target_os = "android"`) versus `cfg(windows)`, so Android takes the same
    code path as Linux. The pure-Rust crates (beads-core, beads-api,
    beads-surface, beads-daemon-core) type-check for `aarch64-linux-android`.
    A full on-device Termux build additionally needs the Android NDK to
    cross-compile the vendored C dependencies (libgit2, SQLite); that remains
    unproven here.

  * The top package is renamed `beads-rs` -> `kopitiam-bds` and is
    `publish = false`. The library keeps the upstream crate name `beads_rs`
    (`[lib] name = "beads_rs"`) so upstream source and future merges are
    undisturbed. The binary is still named `bd`. Sub-crates keep their upstream
    names, carry a `-kopitiam.1` version suffix, and pin their own edition/deps
    (they do NOT inherit KOPITIAM's root workspace) — mirroring crates/kmux.

  * Two upstream sub-crates are NOT carried into the workspace build (excluded
    in the root Cargo.toml): `fuzz` (needs cargo-fuzz/nightly) and
    `beads_stateright_models` (a `stateright` model-checking harness). They
    remain on disk unmodified for provenance.

Upstream's own release/packaging/CI scripts and contributor documentation were
not carried into the fork's build.
